## 6. The Ideal as Logic's Imaginary God

**Definition 4: God (Projected)**

Let \( \mathcal{G}_p \) denote the projected God:

\[
\mathcal{G}_p := \text{An imagined being or principle that is:}
\begin{array}{l}
\text{Perfect} \\
\text{Complete} \\
\text{All-knowing} \\
\text{All-powerful} \\
\text{The source of all truth}
\end{array}
\]

**Theorem 5: The Ideal Functions as an Imaginary God**

\[
\mathcal{I}_d \equiv \mathcal{G}_p
\]

**Proof by Comparison:**

| Aspect | Imaginary God | The Ideal |
|:---|:---|:---|
| Perfection | Absolutely perfect | Absolutely complete |
| Omnipotence | All-powerful | All-derivable |
| Omniscience | All-knowing | All-true |
| Source of Truth | Divine revelation | Axiomatic derivation |
| Worship | Prayer, obedience | Formalization, rigor |
| Salvation | Redemption | Certainty |
| Unattainability | Hidden, mysterious | Approachable, unattainable |

**Corollary 7: The Ideal Is a Theological Projection**

\[
\mathcal{I}_d = \text{Theology projected onto logic}
\]

We have transferred the attributes of God—perfection, completeness, omniscience—onto our formal systems. Logic has become our imaginary God.

